{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nPlanning consumes `requires_create` and `requires_parent` in `ticket_planner.py:74-82,192` and `batch_dispatch.py:524`, but production builders at `run_differs.py:568` and `create_route.py:210-216` do not emit them. A parent and child created in one pass can therefore reach Jira in the wrong order.\n\n## Approach\n\nEmit prerequisite values in the planner’s existing local-ticket-ID namespace. An outbound create already has `Mutation.target == om.local_id`; that local ID is the create provider identity in `TicketPlan.identity`, the key present in `local_snapshot`, and the only usable identity before Jira assigns a key. For every parent/create edge, emit the parent’s local ticket ID in `requires_create` and/or `requires_parent`; never emit a Jira key for a yet-unbound create.\n\nEnsure the parent create advertises that same local-ID target, so `_effective_create_providers` satisfies the dependent in the same pass. A previously existing parent is likewise satisfied by its local ID in `local_snapshot`; an unknown local ID remains dependency-deferred. Keep the current opaque-metadata planner behavior rather than adding a second identity conversion layer.\n\nAt batch dispatch, require the ordered parent create to complete and persist the parent local-ID-to-Jira mapping before executing the child operation. Test the real builder-to-planner-to-dispatch path with a parent and child whose Jira keys are initially absent; prove the child sees the mapping only after its local-ID provider is applied.\n\n## Scope\n\n- production mutation builders and create route\n- planner and batch dispatch dependency consumption\n- planner-shadow and operation-coordinator regression tests\n\n## Testing\n\nCover an unbound parent/child create batch, an already-bound parent, an unknown local parent identity, unrelated creates, and dispatch ordering. Assert local IDs—not Jira keys—at every emitted provider/dependency edge.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Production builders emit applicable `requires_create` and `requires_parent` values as parent local ticket IDs.\n- [ ] Each parent create has `Mutation.target` equal to the same local ID advertised to dependents, so it is an effective provider in that pass.\n- [ ] An unbound parent/child fixture has no Jira keys before planning, retains a local-ID dependency edge, and orders the parent create before the child plan.\n- [ ] An already-bound parent local ID satisfies the dependency without introducing a new create.\n- [ ] An unknown local parent ID leaves the dependent `dependency_deferred` rather than silently treating a Jira key or unrelated identity as available.\n- [ ] Dispatcher coverage proves the child does not apply until the parent create has persisted the matching local-ID-to-Jira mapping.\n- [ ] Unrelated creates retain their current deterministic ordering and no-op behavior."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788376521915020001,"uuid":"59c42aeb-fe01-4e7f-8d69-cb581fda233c"}